at Judy delicious lab at the University  of Virginia while studying children's  understanding of scale researchers  discovered a very surprising phenomenon  involving self-awareness a crucial step  toward becoming a social being  two-and-a-half-year-old Madeleine is  invited to play in a room full of large  toys at this age she already has a sense  of herself and would have passed the  shopping cart test with flying colors  she's given hands-on experience with  each toy in the room researchers wanted  to know what the baby human would do if  they replaced these life-size toys with  perfect miniature replicas how would  they physically relate to them Madeleine  notices that there is something  different about the room now  why don't you pretend like it's the big  one anything get in its never arrived  but that doesn't stop her from treating  the toys as if nothing had changed  ignoring the size of the car she  proceeds to try and get into it as far  as it goes  many other two-and-a-half-year-old  children tested responded in the same  way as Madeleine did they did not modify  their behavior with the toys even though  they could see that they were smaller  so the researchers wondered why would  the baby human who by the age of two and  a half has a sense of themselves in  their own bodies ever relate to the  miniatures as if they were still  full-sized toys it seems that the baby  human wants to play with the toys so  badly it can cause them to momentarily  disregard the difference between the  size of the toy and the size of their  own body here Judi DeLoach has concluded  that at this age the baby humans sense  of scale is so fragile that their  emotions can occasionally override their  perception of themselves but their  perception of their physical self  includes one other major aspect of  becoming a social being and that is  gender
 

